# Your real editor, on a slide

`<code>` blocks are highlighted statically (fast, deterministic). But when you
want your **actual** Neovim — your colorscheme, your Treesitter, your config —
just run it in a `<tty>` block.

Arrow keys to navigate, `q` to quit.

---

```deck:slide
title: Static highlight
```

## The same file, highlighted statically

A `<code>` block renders instantly with Tokyo Night colors — no process, no
startup flash:

<code lang="lua">
-- bootstrap lazy.nvim, LazyVim and your plugins
require("config.lazy")
</code>

Compare it with the next slide, where the very same file is drawn by your
**actual** nvim.

---

```deck:slide
title: init.lua
```

## `~/.config/nvim/init.lua`, rendered by nvim itself

<tty title="init.lua" rows="20" cols="76" prerender keys="\r:lua for _,w in ipairs(vim.api.nvim_list_wins()) do local c=vim.api.nvim_win_get_config(w) if c.relative~='' then pcall(vim.api.nvim_win_close,w,true) end end\rgg">
nvim -R -n -i NONE -c 'autocmd User VeryLazy ++once set laststatus=0 showtabline=0 cmdheight=0 noruler noshowmode signcolumn=no foldcolumn=0 statuscolumn= nonumber norelativenumber nocursorline nocursorcolumn nolist nowrap fillchars=eob:\ | redraw!' "$HOME/.config/nvim/init.lua"
</tty>

The `-c` flag strips nvim's UI chrome; `keys=` feeds keystrokes after startup to
close any popups (LSP progress, notifications) and jump back to the top.

---

# How it works

- `<tty>` runs `$SHELL -lc <command>` in a real pseudo-terminal.
- `keys="…"` sends keystrokes into that PTY a few times during startup
  (`\r`, `\n`, `\t`, `\e` escapes are decoded) — generic enough to drive any
  TUI, not just nvim.
- Nothing in your config is modified: it is opened read-only (`-R`), with no
  swap file and no shada (`-n -i NONE`).
- `prerender` starts nvim at deck load (not on first view) and keeps it alive
  for the session, so the slide is already drawn — no startup flash — when you
  arrive. It is independent of `id`/`persist`: a prerendered process lives for
  the whole session at its own slide, one per occurrence.
